+
+**The ground claim.** A surface that does not know its ground paints the bound. A consumer that knows what it paints under a scope says so with `data-glass-ground` (`src/styles/tokens/glass.css`): `="page"` for the page itself, `="field"` for a substrate that publishes its tones (`<Aurora>` does, on its parent: `data-glass-field`), or bare with `--glass-ground-lo/-hi` written beside it (`glassGroundTone()` turns a colour into both). Inside a claim the content rungs (wash, quiet, resting) thin to the α the claimed ground licenses; chrome, the dock and the dialog stay on the bound. The library never claims.
 
 It deliberately supplies **no position, no z-index, no radius, no size**. A plate is paint, not a box. `.dock-plate` is the worked example: it composes `glass-plate` and declares `position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1` itself, because only the surface knows which box it is lining.
 
